    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, i'm working on adding a small loft to a residential build out in Oregon. i'm looking at using a heavy Douglas Fir beam for the main support. if the span is 14 feet and i'm expecting a live load of about 40 psf plus the dead load of the loft itself, what kind of deflection limits should i be looking at to make sure the floor doesn't feel bouncy? also, what are some things i should look for in the timber itself to make sure it's actually going to hold the weight? thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For residential loft spans, typical live load deflection limits are often $L/360$ or $L/240$ to prevent an overly bouncy feel. With a 14-foot span, this translates to a maximum allowable deflection of roughly between $\\sim 3.9$ inches and $5.8$ inches. When examining the Douglas Fir, always verify the species grade (e.g., No. 2 or stronger) and ensure the timber is kiln-dried to a low moisture content to maintain structural integrity. Additionally, look for minimal knots or defects in the beam that could act as points of stress concentration.",
      "source": "target",
