    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "non-destructive testing sounds a bit overkill for a small loft, but it makes sense if there's stuff going on inside. i guess my main worry is just getting a beam that looks fine on the outside but has some kind of \"secret\" weakness that causes it to fail unexpectedly later on. are there any specific types of grain patterns or subtle irregularities that would be a red flag that something is \"hiding\" in there and could compromise the load capacity?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A red flag to look for, even if subtle, is inconsistent grain density or areas where the grain appears unusually wavy or distorted. Large, tight clusters of knots clustered near the center of the beam can also indicate internal stresses or weaker wood hidden beneath the surface. Additionally, uneven cross-sections or subtle bowing that isn't straight through the entire length should be treated as a potential hidden weakness. Ultimately, the dimensional lumber stamp should confirm the engineered grading standards before installation.",
